  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 PM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nA jet max embedded in the primary mid/upper-level flow will slide \neast later today as a strong shortwave punches across the northern \nGreat Lakes and southern Ontario. The associated surface cyclone \nwill also trek across Ontario today and Wednesday with the attendant \ncold front set to swing down into northern Michigan Wednesday \nmorning/afternoon. Expansive ridging is then set to amplify to our \nwest across much of the center of the continent late this week and \nthrough the upcoming weekend.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nPrimary forecast concern today is the potential for isolated to \nscattered storms initiating across northeast lower along the lake \nbreeze pushing inland from Lake Huron. Confidence is still low in \nwhether storms will form or not over the next few hours -- but any \nstorms that do form will have a high chance of being strong/severe \nwith the greatest threat being large hail. Impressive buoyancy on \nthe order of 2,000+ J/kg MLCAPE overlapped by strong deep-layer \nshear ~45+ kts will support severe thunderstorm potential with any \nconvection that initiates. However, this would largely be driven by \nlow-level forcing via the lake breeze boundary and may be competing \nwith unfavorable subsidence aloft evidenced by subtle height rises \nthrough the afternoon. Current thoughts cast some doubt on \ninitiation as competing forcing could make it difficult. More \naggressive diurnal mixing of low-level moisture may ensue this \nafternoon, creating convective inhibition in the vicinity of the \nboundary -- making it hard for mixing/forcing along the lake breeze \nto extend high enough to initiate storms. Regardless, trends will be \nmonitored closely over the next few hours.\n\nFocus then shifts to late tonight and Wednesday with additional \nrounds of showers and storms across the area along/ahead of the \nfront that drops down into the area. The best chances for multiple \nrounds of showers and potential storms is expected to be along and \nsouth of M-72. Given elevated buoyancy and strong shear, a few \nstrong/severe storms cannot be ruled out. However, unfavorable \nconvective evolution may limit severe potential and the bulk of \nstrongest activity may end of south of the CWA across mid Michigan \nthrough the afternoon. Rain chances look to diminish heading into \nWednesday evening.\n\nLooking ahead, aforementioned ridging building to our west will help \nkeep rain chances low late this week through the holiday weekend. \nRounds of convection are likely to form to our west during this \ntime, current confidence is that the bulk of this activity will ride \nthe ridge and track to our south. After a warm, humid day today, \nmore seasonable temperatures are anticipated through the back half \nof this weekend and upcoming weekend with highs in the mid 70s to \nlow 80s and overnight lows in the 50s and low 60s.",
